\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


A splittable flow may route one demand along several paths; an unsplittable flow routes its entire demand on one path. $d_{\max}=\max_i d_i$, $x_e,y_e$ are edge loads, and $c(x)=\sum_e c_e x_e$.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
Source-record limitation: Resolved by a formally verified counterexample published July 2026: Archive of Formal Proofs, Dinitz_Garg_Goemans_Counterexample, https://isa-afp.org/entries/Dinitz_Garg_Goemans_Counterexample.html. The cost-preserving x+dmax target is refuted.

The historical source snapshot still labels this record open. A July 2026 formally verified counterexample refutes its cost-preserving rounding assertion; this record preserves the source wording and is not presented as an unresolved conjecture.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Given a single-source network with capacities $u$, costs $c$, source $s$, sinks $t_i$, and demands $d_i$ satisfying the no-bottleneck condition $d_{\max}\leq u_e$, does every splittable flow $x$ satisfying the demands admit an unsplittable flow $y$ satisfying the same demands such that $y_e\leq x_e+d_{\max}$ for every edge $e$ and $c(y)\leq c(x)$? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2008/51}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record open; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Round a single-source flow to one path per demand while preserving cost and allowing only the stated additive edge-load increase.

